# Line endings are a deployment concern here, not a style preference.
#
# The release artifact carries shell scripts that root executes on the Linux
# host -- ops/deploy/deploy-blue-diamond and scripts/*.sh. A CRLF shebang makes
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash` into `bash`, which the kernel reports as a missing
# interpreter, so a single contributor with the wrong core.autocrlf could ship a
# deploy script the server cannot run at all.
#
# Several contract tests also assert byte identity -- the FeelStack media
# fixture is pinned by SHA-256 on both sides of the integration. Those hashes
# are computed over LF content, so a CRLF working tree fails them locally while
# CI passes, which trains people to ignore a real class of failure.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is deliberate: it normalizes the WORKING TREE
# too, so a Windows checkout is byte-identical to what CI and the server see.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary -- never transformed, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
